(story title) Ḫattušumna Laḫḫa Tablet 1: The Bakery
(tablet title) Tablet 1: The Bakery

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%% Basic setup and courtyard region
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

(direction numbers enabled)
(given first message)

#me
(current player *)
(name * in #nominative/#bare) ūk
(name * in #genitive) ammel
(name * in #dative/#accusative/#ablative) ammuk
(* is #in #courtyard)

(intro)
	Nu (#dative #courtyard) ešun. %% "I was in the courtyard."
	Nu (#nominative #dog) (#dative #building) pait. %% "A dog went into the building."
	Nu (#nominative #baker) (#ablative #building) uet. %% "The baker came out of the building."
	Nu (#nominative #baker) (#dative #washhouse) pait. %% "The baker went into the washhouse."
	(now) (update needed)

(tutorial)
	~(tutorial introduced)
	(div @tutorial) {
		Welcome to tablet 2! The grammar is going to get more complicated this time, but if you made it through tablet 1, you should be well-prepared for what comes next. (par)
		In particular, nouns will now appear in two additional cases: the "genitive" for one noun modifying another, and the "ablative" for the source of a movement or the tool used for an action. Verbs will sometimes describe what other people are doing instead of just you yourself. And some words will be written with "logograms": special cuneiform signs that represent whole words, like how "7" is short for "seven". When this happens, the names of those cuneiform signs will be written in CAPITAL LETTERS separated by dots. (par)
		Don't worry, though; you can still click any word to see its definition, and the basic vocabulary isn't going anywhere. Good luck! You've got this!
	}
	(now) (tutorial introduced)

%% We're going to need so many of these, we should handle it right at the top
(room *(storage $))
(*(storage $) uses preverb #in)
(name (storage $Obj) in #nominative/#genitive) (contents $Obj) antakaš
(name (storage $Obj) in #dative) (contents $Obj) antaki
(name (storage $Obj) in #accusative) (contents $Obj) antakan
(name (storage $Obj) in #ablative) (contents $Obj) antakaz
(name (storage $Obj) in #bare) (contents $Obj) antaka
(short name (storage $Obj)) (contents $Obj)
(medium short name (storage $Obj)) (#dative $Obj)

